  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "If the vertex of the conic $y^{2}-4 y=4 x-4 a$ always lies between the straight lines $x+y=3$ and $2 x+2 y-1=0 .$ then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "w5FMJ",
      "text": "$2    < a < 4$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "LFkiT",
      "text": "$-\\frac{1}{2}    < a < 2$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "5ezCy",
      "text": "$0    < a < 2$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "mRhgz",
      "text": "$-\\frac{1}{2}    < a < \\frac{3}{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "We have, $y^{2}-4 y=4 x-4 a$ $\\Rightarrow \\quad(y-2)^{2}-4=4 x-4 a$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad(y-2)^{2}=4 x-4 a+4$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad(y-2)^{2}=4|x-(a-1)|$\r\nHence, vertex is $(a-1,2)$ \r\nSince, vertex lies between the lines $x+y=3$ and $2 x+2 y-1=0,$ then\r\n$(a-1+2-3)(2 a-2+4-1)    < 0$\r\n$\\Rightarrow(a-2)(2 a+1) < 0 \\Rightarrow a \\in\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\right)$",
    "image": null
  },
